1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,976
They printed my death notice forty-eight hours before I even touched the steering wheel of that truck.

2
00:00:06,276 --> 00:00:16,140
I found the leather-bound ledger hidden deep inside my grandfather's locked mahogany desk, smelling heavily of aged dust and dried ink.

3
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:23,832
Every single name listed in these heavy pages belonged to someone who died exactly when the paper predicted.

4
00:00:24,132 --> 00:00:30,204
I laughed it off as some twisted family prank until I saw my own full name staring back.

5
00:00:30,504 --> 00:00:38,904
Leo Vance. Fatal brake failure on Maple Street bridge. Tuesday morning, precisely at eight thirty-two.

6
00:00:39,204 --> 00:00:47,772
I refused to drive. I stayed locked inside my apartment all morning, watching the clock hands creep past the hour.

7
00:00:48,072 --> 00:00:57,288
When eight thirty-two finally arrived and passed in complete silence, a massive wave of relief crashed over my chest.

8
00:00:57,588 --> 00:01:04,284
I walked outside to grab the morning paper, laughing at how foolish I had been to trust a cursed book.

9
00:01:04,584 --> 00:01:11,256
That is when the delivery boy handed me a fresh envelope containing tomorrow's date and a brand new headline.

10
00:01:11,556 --> 00:01:20,460
The words printed inside formed a question: If reading your own obituary triggers the end, who just turned the final page?
